A node carries a sid, given to it once and never changed. It is cited by its sid everywhere: in the links between nodes, in the code, in the tests and in commits.

Its location names where it is in the specification: the part it is in, followed by its place there. A term's place is the term's name. Any other node's place is its number among the nodes under the same parent that are not terms. Beside this:

- a domain's header is located by the domain's name alone;
- the node at the top of a file of the guidelines, of the technical appendix or of contracts is located by the part's name and the file's;
- each file of the guidelines and of the appendix numbers its nodes under its top node's location;
- the part contracts, like a domain, is numbered under the part's name straight through its files.

The location is computed from where the node is, never written by hand. Nothing cites a node by its location.

A node's status is draft or approved. That of a section and of a domain's header is built from the nodes under it (§s-9kfdycuq34beqvs). What follows holds of every other node.

Approved means that one of the node's owners (§s-67vekyup7y8smap) approved its present wording: its title, text and reason, and the values its kind adds beside them, such as the fields of a RecordType. Otherwise the node is a draft.

Any change to that wording, an owner's own included, makes the node a draft until one of its owners approves the wording it then holds. So does a withdrawal of its approval, which names whoever made it. Anyone may withdraw the approval of a node outside the SpecSheriff guidelines a project takes (§s-bsya3febm6u64ab), but that of the rule that approves the specification (§s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2).

The status stays as it is through a change to:

- the node's links or its metadata;
- who its owners are;
- what is built into it from other nodes, such as the values gathered beside a field (§s-ju5vcanbjph7mgh);
- where it stands.

A rule is bound to its implementation by three records:

- a SpecTag carrying the node's sid, on the symbol in the code that implements it;
- a SpecTag carrying the node's sid, declared by the test written from it where a run of the tests reports it;
- in the node's metadata, the lists of that code and those tests.

All three are written by hand, and they must agree.
